@node pcmciacheck___PCMCIA_CF_Hardware_Test_Tool "pcmciacheck - PCMCIA/CF Hardware Test Tool"
@{b}@{u}pcmciacheck - PCMCIA/CF Hardware Test Tool@{uu}@{ub}

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Main}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Overview}

@endnode

@node Overview "Overview"
@{b}Overview@{ub}

@{fg shine}pcmciacheck@{fg text} is a diagnostic tool for testing PCMCIA CompactFlash card
compatibility with Amiga systems. It tests the same read/write modes
used by the @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} driver to determine which data access
modes work correctly with your CF card, helping identify potential
compatibility issues before using the card with the @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text}
driver.

  * @{b}Multiple Access Mode Testing@{ub} : Tests 5 different read modes and 5 write modes
    (including memory-mapped)
  * @{b}IDENTIFY Read Stability@{ub} : Reads the same sector several times per mode and
    compares the results, the check the driver applies before it accepts a card
  * @{b}Pattern-Based Compatibility Testing@{ub} : Mirrors the @{fg shine}cfd.s@{fg text} driver's RWTest
    functionality
  * @{b}Safe Operation@{ub} : Write testing requires explicit @{fg shine}-w@{fg text} flag to prevent accidental
    data loss
  * @{b}Detailed Progress Reporting@{ub} : Real-time status output suitable for serial
    redirection
  * @{b}IFF Log Format@{ub}: Creates structured log files for analysis
  * @{b}Hardware Detection@{ub}: Automatic card presence detection and timeout handling

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link pcmciacheck___PCMCIA_CF_Hardware_Test_Tool}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Usage}

@endnode

@node Usage "Usage"
@{b}Usage@{ub}

@{fg highlight}
  pcmciacheck [-w] [-s <speed>] <logfile>
  pcmciacheck -identify [speed] [-r <runs>]
  pcmciacheck -cis [speed]
@{fg text}

The tool has three modes. The default run exercises the transfer modes
and writes an IFF log. @{fg shine}-identify@{fg text} and @{fg shine}-cis@{fg text} are focused checks that
print to the console and write nothing.

Options:
  * @{b}@{fg shine}<logfile>@{fg text}@{ub}: Output file for test results (IFF format)
  * @{b}@{fg shine}-w@{fg text}@{ub}: Enable write testing (WARNING: overwrites sectors 1-5 on the CF card)
  * @{b}@{fg shine}-s <speed>@{fg text}@{ub}: Gayle PCMCIA timing to use for the run, @{fg shine}100@{fg text}, @{fg shine}150@{fg text},
  @{fg shine}250@{fg text} or @{fg shine}720@{fg text} nanoseconds, or @{fg shine}all@{fg text} to repeat the capture at each of them
and log every timing. Cannot be combined with @{fg shine}-w@{fg text} . The previous setting
is
  restored on exit. See
  @{"Trying other Gayle timings" link trying_other_gayle_timings}.
  * @{b}@{fg shine}-identify [speed] [-r <runs>]@{fg text}@{ub}: Read IDENTIFY several times per mode
  and compare the blocks, the check the driver makes before it accepts a
  card. Console only. The optional speed is @{fg shine}100@{fg text}, @{fg shine}150@{fg text}, @{fg shine}250@{fg text}, @{fg shine}720@{fg text}, or
  @{fg shine}all@{fg text} to test at each of them and print a matrix. @{fg shine}-r@{fg text} sets the runs per
  mode, 2 to 32, default 5. See
  @{"Checking data path read stability" link checking_data_path_read_stability}.
  * @{b}@{fg shine}-cis [speed]@{fg text}@{ub}: Dump the PCMCIA Card Information Structure (CIS) tuples from
  attribute memory and exit. Read-only, does not use @{fg shine}card.resource@{fg text} or
  @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text}, so it can be used to inspect cards that hang the
  regular driver path. The optional @{fg shine}speed@{fg text} is @{fg shine}100@{fg text}, @{fg shine}150@{fg text}, @{fg shine}250@{fg text} or @{fg shine}720@{fg text}
and overrides the Gayle PCMCIA memory timing for the duration of the
scan
  only; the previous setting is restored on exit. Without it the current
  setting is used.

Examples:

@{b}Read-only testing (safe):@{ub}
@{fg highlight}
  pcmciacheck RAM:test.log
@{fg text}

@{b}Full testing with write verification:@{ub}
@{fg highlight}
  pcmciacheck -w RAM:test.log
@{fg text}

@{b}Serial output redirection:@{ub}
@{fg highlight}
  pcmciacheck -w >SER: RAM:test.log
@{fg text}

@{b}Dump CIS tuples (no driver interaction):@{ub}
@{fg highlight}
  pcmciacheck -cis
  pcmciacheck -cis >RAM:card.cis
@{fg text}

The CIS dump decodes the common tuples (@{fg shine}CISTPL_DEVICE@{fg text}, @{fg shine}CISTPL_FUNCID@{fg text},
@{fg shine}CISTPL_FUNCE@{fg text}, @{fg shine}CISTPL_VERS_1@{fg text}, @{fg shine}CISTPL_MANFID@{fg text}, ...) and hex-dumps any
others. Useful for diagnosing cards that the driver mis-identifies, or
that cause the driver's probe path to hang.

Sample @{fg shine}CISTPL_DEVICE@{fg text} decode:

@{fg highlight}
  0x000: 0x01 CISTPL_DEVICE (length=4)
      type=0xD (FUNCSPEC), wps=1, speed=0x1 (250ns)
      size=0x00000800 (2048 B), size_code=0x01  (units=1, unit=0x1=2K)
@{fg text}

The device-info byte carries the device type in bits 7-4, the
write-protect-switch flag in bit 3, and the speed in bits 2-0. Speed
code @{fg shine}0x7@{fg text} means the real access time is in one or more extended speed
bytes that sit between the device-info byte and the size byte, so those
cards decode like this:

@{fg highlight}
  0x000: 0x01 CISTPL_DEVICE (length=4)
      type=0xD (FUNCSPEC), wps=1, speed=0x7 (extended)
      ext-speed=0x4A (400 ns)
      size=0x00000800 (2048 B), size_code=0x01  (units=1, unit=0x1=2K)
@{fg text}

The @{fg shine}size=@{fg text} line is the decoded byte count (units * unit-size); it
matches the value the OS @{fg shine}DeviceTuple@{fg text} call stores in cfd's
@{fg shine}CFU_DTSize@{fg text}. @{fg shine}size_code@{fg text} is the raw CIS byte for cross-reference.

@{fg shine}CISTPL_DEVICE_OC@{fg text} describes the same device under other operating
conditions and starts with an extra "other conditions" byte, which is
reported separately before the device info:

@{fg highlight}
  0x006: 0x1C CISTPL_DEVICE_OC (length=4)
      other-cond=0x02 (Vcc=X.XV, mwait=0)
      type=0xD (FUNCSPEC), wps=1, speed=0x1 (250ns)
      size=0x00000800 (2048 B), size_code=0x01  (units=1, unit=0x1=2K)
@{fg text}

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Overview}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Checking_CIS_read_stability}

@endnode

@node Checking_CIS_read_stability "Checking CIS read stability"
@{b}Checking CIS read stability@{ub}

CIS is static data in the card's attribute memory, so running
@{fg shine}pcmciacheck -cis@{fg text} several times in succession (without removing or
re-inserting the card) should produce **byte-for-byte identical
output every time**. If consecutive runs disagree, the PCMCIA
attribute-memory read path is producing random bit-flips, and the
driver's CIS gate may reject the card on a corrupted run even though
its I/O path is unaffected.

Observed so far on the @{b}A1200 + ACA1234@{ub} combination with certain
CF cards (notably Transcend TS4GCF133 with manfid=0x004F). Bare A1200
and ACA1240 / ACA1260 on the same card are unaffected. Gayle's PCMCIA
memory-speed register (@{fg shine}$DAB000@{fg text} bits 2-3) does not remediate this:
100ns, 150ns, 250ns, and 720ns all produce the same instability.

@{b}Stable run@{ub} (a healthy reading; every run looks like this):

@{fg highlight}
  0x017: 0x15 CISTPL_VERS_1 (length=24)
      major=4, minor=1
      string[0]: "TRANSCEND"
      string[1]: "TS4GCF133"
  0x031: 0x21 CISTPL_FUNCID (length=2)
      function=0x04 (FIXED_DISK)
      sysinit=0x01
  0x035: 0x22 CISTPL_FUNCE (FUNCEXT) (length=2)
      extension_type=0x01 (Disk Interface), interface=0x01 (IDE)
@{fg text}

@{b}Unstable runs@{ub} (same card, same insertion; three further
consecutive runs, excerpts showing the kind of corruption seen):

FUNCID length byte flipped (0x02 to 0x00), making the parser walk
into the data and treat the next bytes as fresh tuple codes:

@{fg highlight}
  0x031: 0x21 CISTPL_FUNCID (length=0)
  0x033: 0x04 (unknown) (length=1)
      data: 22
  0x036: 0x02 (unknown) (length=1)
      data: 01
@{fg text}

VERS_1 strings garbled by random bit flips:

@{fg highlight}
  0x017: 0x15 CISTPL_VERS_1 (length=24)
      major=0, minor=1
      string[0]: "TPA@@S@@E@@D"
      string[1]: "T"
      string[2]: "4GCF133"
@{fg text}

FUNCEXT interface byte flipped from IDE (0x01) to undefined (0x00),
which is enough to make a strict CIS gate refuse the card:

@{fg highlight}
  0x035: 0x22 CISTPL_FUNCE (FUNCEXT) (length=2)
      extension_type=0x01 (Disk Interface), interface=0x00 ((undefined))
@{fg text}

When opening an issue about a card that the driver mis-identifies or
rejects, capture and attach @{b}several @{fg shine}-cis@{fg text} runs back-to-back@{ub} so
the failure mode is visible. A single dump cannot distinguish "card's
CIS is genuinely malformed" from "attribute-memory reads are unstable
on this host"; the multi-run capture can.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Usage}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Checking_data_path_read_stability}

@endnode

@node Checking_data_path_read_stability "Checking data path read stability"
@{b}Checking data path read stability@{ub}
@{i}Subsections:@{ui}
  @{"  Only the driver's own mode decides  " link Only_the_driver_s_own_mode_decides}

The read-mode test only tells you that the PCMCIA path works at all: the
card accepted IDENTIFY and a block came back. It does not look at the
content. A card can pass every read mode and still hand over different
bytes on every read, and that is what corrupts filesystems.

IDENTIFY is static data on the card, so reading it repeatedly through
the
same mode has to return the same bytes every time. That is what
@{fg shine}-identify@{fg text} does, @{fg shine}-r <runs>@{fg text} times per mode, comparing the blocks:

@{fg highlight}
  pcmciacheck -identify
  pcmciacheck -identify -r 20
  pcmciacheck -identify 720
  pcmciacheck -identify all
@{fg text}

It is its own mode rather than part of the default run because it is the
check worth repeating on a suspect card, and raising the run count or
trying another timing should not mean sitting through the read-mode,
pattern and write tests again. Nothing is written to disk.

The test reports the same two failures the driver rejects a card for:

@{fg highlight}
  [CFD] Getting IDE ID ... FAILED (repeated pattern 8A84)
  [CFD] Getting IDE ID ... FAILED (data mismatch)
@{fg text}

A healthy card:

@{fg highlight}
  Testing IDENTIFY read stability (5 runs per mode)...
    mode 0 (word +8           ) STABLE    5/5 identical, xor 3C11, 0 repeated word(s)
    mode 1 (word +8 stride 16 ) STABLE    5/5 identical, xor 3C11, 0 repeated word(s)
    mode 2 (byte +8 / +8 odd  ) STABLE    5/5 identical, xor 3C11, 0 repeated word(s)
    mode 3 (word +8 read-twice) STABLE    5/5 identical, xor 3C11, 0 repeated word(s)
    mode 4 (MMAP word         ) STABLE    5/5 identical, xor 3C11, 0 repeated word(s)
    Driver uses mode 0: stable
@{fg text}

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Checking_CIS_read_stability}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Only_the_driver_s_own_mode_decides}

@endnode

@node Only_the_driver_s_own_mode_decides "Only the driver's own mode decides"
@{i}Only the driver's own mode decides@{ui}

The driver tries read modes 0, 1, 2 and 3 in order and falls back to
mode 4 last, so it uses the first one that works. A mode further down
that list is never reached once an earlier one works, and a failure
there says nothing about the card.

The report therefore names the mode the driver would use and judges the
card on that one alone:

@{fg highlight}
    mode 4 (MMAP word         ) NOT USABLE (all zeros, nothing was read)
    Driver uses mode 0: stable
    1 other mode(s) are not usable here, which does not
    affect the card: the driver never falls back to them.
@{fg text}

That is a working card. It is common for read mode 4 to be unusable,
since memory-mapped access is not wired up the same way on every
adapter.

A block that comes back entirely zero is reported as `NOT USABLE (all
zeros)` rather than as a repeated pattern: nothing arrived at all, which
is a different fault from a port latched at a value. The driver does not
draw that distinction, its own check would call an all-zero block a
repeated pattern, but the driver is judging a card while this line is
describing one mode.

A stuck data port, returning one word for every read:

@{fg highlight}
    mode 0 (word +8           ) STUCK     repeated pattern 0x8A84 (ATA 0x848A), 255 repeated word(s)
    Driver uses mode 0: NOT stable
    The card is not readable in this setup; the driver
    rejects it with "FAILED (repeated pattern)".
@{fg text}

Repeating the read cannot find this on its own, because a stuck port is
perfectly repeatable: five reads give five identical blocks. It is
therefore checked separately, and before the comparison, in the same
order the driver checks it. The @{fg shine}xor 0000@{fg text} such a card produces is a
giveaway as well, since a block of identical words folds to zero.

An unstable data path:

@{fg highlight}
    mode 0 (word +8           ) UNSTABLE
       5/5 read, 1/5 identical, 5 distinct checksum(s)
       first difference at word 1, up to 126 of 256 words differ
       33 repeated word(s) in the reference block
       xor: 6D97 3C11 7F3F 0ED5 F396
@{fg text}

How to read that:

  * @{b}identical@{ub} is the verdict. Anything below the full run count means
  the same sector read back differently, so the data path is losing
  integrity.
  * @{b}distinct checksum(s)@{ub} is the number of different XOR folds seen. It
  can under-count, since two different blocks can fold to the same
  value.
  * @{b}first difference at word N@{ub} is where the blocks start to diverge.
  * @{b}repeated word(s)@{ub} counts words equal to the one before them,
  ignoring the @{fg shine}0x0000@{fg text} and @{fg shine}0x2020@{fg text} padding. A high count means whole
  words are being repeated, which happens when the data port does not
  advance to the next word. This describes @{i}how@{ui} the block is corrupted;
  it is not the verdict, because a healthy card can contain repeated
  words of its own.
  * @{b}repeated pattern 0xNNNN@{ub} is the stuck value as it was read from the
  port, the same number the driver prints. The value in brackets is the
  byte-swapped one, which is what the word means as ATA data.
  * @{b}xor@{ub} lists the per-run folds so they can be compared with the
  driver's own numbers.

A mode that is not usable at all reports @{fg shine}NO DATA@{fg text}.

Errors are random by nature, so a clean result is not proof. Raise
@{fg shine}-r@{fg text} and run the tool a few times before concluding a card is fine.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Checking_data_path_read_stability}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Trying_other_Gayle_timings}

@endnode

@node Trying_other_Gayle_timings "Trying other Gayle timings"
@{b}Trying other Gayle timings@{ub}

Both the default run and @{fg shine}-identify@{fg text} accept a Gayle PCMCIA timing
(@{fg shine}$DAB000@{fg text} bits 2-3) to use, and restore the previous setting on exit:
@{fg shine}-s <speed>@{fg text} for the default run, a speed argument for @{fg shine}-identify@{fg text}.

Both also accept @{fg shine}all@{fg text}, and the two answer different questions:

  @{b}@{ub}                              @{b}what it gives you@{ub}                      
  ----------------------------  -------------------------------------  
  @{fg shine}pcmciacheck -identify all@{fg text}     the verdict per mode and timing, as a  
                                matrix on the console                  
  @{fg shine}pcmciacheck -s all <logfile>@{fg text}  the raw IDENTIFY blocks for every      
                                timing, in the log                     

Use the first to see whether timing is the variable. Use the second when
the answer needs to be analysed later or attached to a bug report, since
the log then carries the actual bytes each mode returned at each timing,
not just a verdict. @{fg shine}-s all@{fg text} cannot be combined with @{fg shine}-w@{fg text}: rewriting
sectors 1 to 5 once per timing gains nothing, and the mode the read-back
uses can differ between timings.

@{fg shine}-identify all@{fg text} runs the stability test at each of the four timings and
prints a matrix:

@{fg highlight}
  pcmciacheck -identify all
  
  Testing IDENTIFY read stability at each Gayle timing (5 runs per mode)...
            100ns   150ns   250ns   720ns
    mode 0  UNSTAB  UNSTAB  UNSTAB  STABLE  <- driver uses this
    mode 1  UNSTAB  UNSTAB  UNSTAB  STABLE
    mode 2  STABLE  STABLE  STABLE  STABLE
    mode 3  UNSTAB  UNSTAB  UNSTAB  STABLE
    mode 4  ZEROS   ZEROS   ZEROS   ZEROS
    Gayle timing restored to 250ns
    Mode 0 is stable only at: 720ns
    The card cannot follow the faster settings. Note the
    driver programs the timing itself, this cannot pin it.
@{fg text}

The verdicts are abbreviated so the columns line up: @{fg shine}STABLE@{fg text}, @{fg shine}UNSTAB@{fg text},
@{fg shine}STUCK@{fg text}, @{fg shine}ZEROS@{fg text}, @{fg shine}NODATA@{fg text}. The modes are the ones listed under
@{"Read Mode Results" link read_mode_results}.

A card that becomes stable at a slower timing is being driven faster
than it can follow. A matrix where every column reads the same says
timing is not the variable, which is just as useful to know. As in the
single-run report, only the driver's own row decides the verdict.

Two things to keep in mind:

  * @{b}This is a diagnostic, not a fix.@{ub} The driver programs the same
  register itself through @{fg shine}card.resource@{fg text}, so an override here lasts
  only while @{fg shine}pcmciacheck@{fg text} runs. A card that only works at 720ns in this
  test will not thereby work under the driver.
  * **@{fg shine}$DAB000@{fg text} is documented as the PCMCIA @{i}memory@{ui} timing**, and read
  modes 0 to 3 use I/O space. It is possible for those rows not to move
  at all across the sweep. Read mode 4 uses common memory and is the one
  most likely to respond.

A sweep issues four times as many IDENTIFY commands as a normal run, so
lower @{fg shine}-r@{fg text} if it takes too long. Past experience is mixed: the CIS
instability documented above was not remediated by any of the four
settings.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Only_the_driver_s_own_mode_decides}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Test_Results_Interpretation}

@endnode

