@node Error_Conditions "Error Conditions"
@{i}Error Conditions@{ui}

  * @{b}"No card inserted"@{ub}: PCMCIA card not detected
  * @{b}"Cannot open timer.device"@{ub}: System resource issue
  * @{b}"Cannot allocate memory"@{ub}: Insufficient RAM
  * @{b}"TIMEOUT"@{ub}: Card not responding to commands

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Multi_Sector_Issue_Detection}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Log_File_Format}

@endnode

@node Log_File_Format "Log File Format"
@{b}Log File Format@{ub}

The tool creates IFF-format log files with the following chunks:

  * @{b}FORM/pcc3@{ub}: Main container
  * @{b}gspd@{ub}: Gayle timing this group used, two big-endian words: the speed in
  nanoseconds, and 1 when @{fg shine}-s@{fg text} chose it or 0 when it is the system's own
  setting
  * @{b}rdc0-rdc4@{ub} : IDENTIFY data for each read mode, 512 bytes each, or empty when
    the mode returned nothing
  * @{b}ptst@{ub}: Pattern test results (2 bytes)
  * @{b}wcln@{ub}: Write byte counts (20 bytes, 5 modes x 4, with -w flag)
  * @{b}wcda@{ub}: Write read-back data (with -w flag)

@{fg shine}gspd@{fg text} starts a timing group: everything up to the next @{fg shine}gspd@{fg text} was
captured at the timing it names. A normal run writes one group, about
2.6 KB. @{fg shine}-s all@{fg text} writes four, one per timing, about 10.5 KB, so a reader
walking the chunks in order sees @{fg shine}gspd rdc0..rdc4 ptst@{fg text} four times.

@{fg shine}-identify@{fg text} and @{fg shine}-cis@{fg text} write no log at all, they only print.

The container type is @{fg shine}pcc3@{fg text} because the read modes now mirror the
driver's transfer modes. Per-mode blocks in a @{fg shine}pcc2@{fg text} log came from
different register addresses and are not comparable with these.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Error_Conditions}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Serial_Line_Usage}

@endnode

@node Serial_Line_Usage "Serial Line Usage"
@{b}Serial Line Usage@{ub}

For remote monitoring or headless systems, redirect output to serial:

@{fg highlight}
  pcmciacheck -w >SER: RAM:test.log
@{fg text}

The progress output uses @{fg shine}\r\n@{fg text} line endings and structured formatting
optimized for serial terminals.

